Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?feat(food):=20size=20the=20plate,=20add=20the?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20hidden=20fat=20and=20compute=20kcal=20from=20grams=20=C3=97?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20per=20100=20g?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Photo-based calorie estimates were the largest error in the energy balance: published
evaluations of vision LLMs on meal photos sit around 36 % MAPE per meal in energy, with large
portions underestimated and hidden fat as the main miss (Fridolfsson et al. 2025; Cinar et al.
2026; Nutrients 2025 ChatGPT-5 context study). The levers those studies found are now in the
request:

- The item schema is grams plus composition per 100 g; parseItem multiplies them, so the
  amount (the uncertain half) is separated from the composition (table data the model knows).
  A reply in the older per-portion shape still reads.
- The prompt walks the dietitian's steps: every food including hidden layers, amounts judged
  against known objects (plate 26 cm, cutlery 19 cm, hand 18 cm) and never a typical serving,
  cooking fat and sauces listed as their own item with default amounts (olive oil in Spain),
  then the composition. The notes end with the one thing the photo cannot show and its rough
  effect in kcal, so the wearer can correct it.
- output_config.effort "high": Opus 5.5 defaults to medium and portion sizing is the hard part.
  A model that rejects output_config still falls back to the plain request as before.

The stored food log is unchanged: FoodItem keeps its per-portion numbers.

